On Mon, 15 Dec 2008, Ray Dillinger wrote:

> So, it's been 5 years since the latest nethack release.
> The Devteam is hard to communicate with.
> There may never be another version.
> But that in itself isn't a disaster.

Nice start, and all true.

> Or is nethack finally complete?

That's a tough one. If you mean "will players have fun with Nethack in 
fifteen years", then the answer is Yes. The answer is also Yes if you 
restrict to players active already now. However, if you look at the number 
of patches and ideas around (more below), then the answer (to some!) is 
No.

> If, fifteen years from now, 3.4.3 is still the current
> version, do we lose anything?

To be brutally honest, as a developer of another product on the market (I 
don't think in these terms, actually): Crawl benefits from Nethack's 
development. being stalled, other roguelikes do too, of course. Nethack 
has shortcomings whose non-fixing will help other games, including Crawl. 
So yes, you lose players.

Something more concrete: Nethack is behind the times when it comes to the 
interface. I've lost count on how often I've read these lines on r.g.r.n:
  newbie: "It would sure help if they'd make hp losses more visible."
  oldbie: "It is the player's task to watch hp all the time."
This is backwards design and just one example.

> The game has already advanced to the point where virtually all players, 
> even players that have been playing the same version for five years and 
> played other versions for seven years before that, are still in their
> learning curve.

I think this is wrong, in a somewhat objective sense. As far as I 
understood, the best players can win Nethack in a rather unconditional 
way. This means that the learning curve is done with. In Derek's words, 
Nethack is solved. You can still have fun with it, especially if you play 
naively (i.e. non-paranoid) or even unspoiled. You can treat it as a 
puzzle game, or you can try to write bots. But the game has been mastered.

> Nobody needs a new version of chess.

I don't think that's the proper comparison, sorry :)

> Maybe we should start looking at the possibility that
> nethack exists as it is, and will continue to exist as it
> is into the indefinite future, and that that isn't necessarily
> a disaster.

No, definitely not a desaster, but sure a lost opportunity. Perceptions of 
the notions "fun" and "game" differ between individuals, but let me
mention my take on the main difference between Nethack and Crawl:
   In Nethack, fully spoiled players can win every game with statistical 
certainty. Unspoiled players will not win. (Yes, I know Krysia's story.)
   In Crawl, even the best players will die sometimes. Unspoiled players do 
win. Good players win more often.

In my world, Nethack lost its "game appeal" by being solved. In concrete 
terms, being solved also implies these:
* You can pick up the game, read spoilers along the way, and win. (I don't
think it is possible to learn the techniques necessary for permanent 
winning in this manner, though.)
* There is no room for comparison of the best players. They just win. You 
can take length of streak as measure, but then you are just comparing 
amounts of free time and paranoia levels.

I am as curious as everyone else, and I sure would love to know about the 
devteam's opinion on these topics. (In particular, if winnability from the 
start was a set design goal, or an accident.) However, we will never know, 
and the reason could simply be that no one cares anymore.

On the other hand, I think that the OP is right: the current state is not 
ideal. Writing to r.g.r.n about this just won't help any. I said it 
before, and I'll say it again: there was no talk so far about any 
game-specific features, patches, deficits - this is bad! In my opinion, a 
good way to start would be playing Sporkhack until you won a couple of 
times, and post your opinion. This way you could get a sub-community 
interested in improving Nethack and lively discussing details. Oh, it 
already exists on #sporlhack :)

